    "editorialMarkdown": "## Head insertion reverses order\n\nA new head points at the old head, so the most recently added first value is always first in the chain. Repeating that operation is the same core pointer move used by an in-place linked-list reversal: redirect one next link at a time.\n\nThe trap most solvers hit is treating an empty structure as though it has a valid first or last slot. That often passes ordinary examples, then fails only after a long sequence of removals. Keep the element count as the source of truth and check it before reading or moving a pointer.\n\nEach addFirst and removeFirst is O(1) time; toArray is O(n) time; total storage is O(n) space.",
