    "editorialMarkdown": "The intended approach requires defining a custom Node class with `value` and `next` properties, maintaining `head` and `tail` pointers alongside an element counter. For `addFirst`, create a new node pointing to the current head, and update the tail if the list was empty. For `addLast`, attach a new node to the current tail's `next` reference and update the tail pointer. `removeFirst` updates the head to the second node, remembering to return the extracted value and handle the case where the list becomes empty by also clearing the tail. The `get` and `toArray` methods traverse the sequence starting from the head. This pattern is the foundational Singly Linked List.\n\nThe time complexity is O(1) for `addFirst`, `addLast` (with a tail pointer), `removeFirst`, and `size`. `get` and `toArray` take O(n) time. The space complexity is O(n) to store the nodes.\n\nThe one trap most solvers hit is neglecting to update the tail pointer when the sequence transitions between zero and one elements. For example, when removing the last remaining node via `removeFirst`, failing to set the tail pointer to null will cause subsequent `addLast` operations to link new nodes to a discarded, unreachable node, breaking the sequence entirely.",
